# Line endings are decided here, not by whoever's editor touched the file last.
# This repo is developed on both Windows and Linux, so without these rules the
# bytes on disk depend on the checkout machine and a one-character edit can show
# up as a whole-file diff.
#
# `*.sh` is the one that has already caused a bug. A CRLF shebang is not a style
# issue: the kernel hands `#!/usr/bin/env python3\r` to env, which then looks for
# an interpreter literally named `python3\r`, fails with exit 127 and writes no
# artifact — so the symptom reads as "every query failed" rather than "the
# interpreter line is broken" (docs/ops.md §7, "CRLF shebang"). Forcing LF here
# means the script that reaches disk is the script that was committed.
#
# `*.py`, `*.ts` and `*.md` are LF for the same class of reason without the
# shebang: a CRLF checkout still compiles, but it makes every subsequent diff
# noisy and every patch context line a coin flip on a repo with two platforms.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.py    text eol=lf
*.ts    text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf

# The demo's rendered stills are pixels. Marking them binary tells git to stop
# guessing: no eol conversion, and no attempt to diff or merge them as text.
demo/*.png    binary
